          "en": "Symmetric encryption is fast but raises the key-sharing problem. Asymmetric encryption solves the\nsharing but is slow and only encrypts small messages. In practice, both are used together:\n\n1. The sender generates a random, single-use symmetric key (the *session key*).\n2. The sender encrypts the message, whatever its size, with that key (AES-256-GCM).\n3. The sender encrypts **only the session key** with the recipient's public key.\n4. The recipient decrypts the session key with the private key, then the message.\n\nThis scheme is called **hybrid encryption**. You use it every day without seeing it: HTTPS (TLS),\nSSH, end-to-end encrypted messaging, PGP and the \"envelope\" encryption of cloud KMS services all\nrely on this idea. The next lessons detail each building block: hashing, AES, TLS, certificates\nand then key management.\n"
